ederal charge  and went to prison in that case the feds  and the local prosecutor worked together  the investigations can be lengthy you  need to have  the federal agency go out and more  often than not re-interview every  witness  with an eye towards the elements of that  federal charge and then you have to  think about  are there other witnesses that you could  possibly investigate  and talk to that are related in the  wells case glassman says that officer  lied about the use of force  federal civil rights cases aren't often  brought  the willful standard makes it very  difficult to prove these cases  it's a higher standard than just knowing  it's  essentially saying that the the  defendant violated the constitution on  purpose  like you knew what reasonable force was  and  even though you knew that you on purpose  used excessive force  but glassman says those cases can be  successfully prosecuted  these are cases that it's incredibly  important to bring if you are  pro law enforcement because officers  who commit these types of offenses  tarnish the badge  now ben glassman tells me that another  reason that these investigations can  take so long is because experts in use  of force are often consulted  and the agents investigating these cases  really have to take a close look at the  training these officers to receive  when determining whether or not the  force used is excessive and violates the  law  brian thanks angeannette be sure to tune  in to the law and crime network for  capital gavel coverage  of shroven's trial starting march 8th  when we come back  former nfl tight end kellen winslow ii  is sentenced for his multiple sex crimes  hear what his victims had to say after  the break  former nfl player kellen winslow ii was  sentenced to 14 years in prison  for a series of rapes and a slew of  other sex crimes  the now 37 year old was supposed to be  sentenced in march of 2020  but that was delayed due to kova 19.  winslow was convicted for raping a 58  year old homeless woman  in 2019 as well as indecent exposure and  a lewd public act  he was going to be retried on sex  felonies including the 2003 rape of a 17  year old but he later pled guilty before  standing trial  mr wenzel has taken so much from me that  finally being able to point him out  and make it here what he's done makes me  feel empowered  and that every victim should know that  they have a voice as well  i feel that with everything that mr  wenzel has done she should be sentenced  before  14th it may not be like but at least we  can keep them away from harming anyone  your honor i was so scared when he raped  me  that every black jeep that drove by  might be him  i was scared to go outside not knowing  who it was  i can't believe he could be so nasty it  really hurt my feelings that he took  advantage of me  he scared me so bad knowing now that he  was my neighbor  and i couldn't believe that a nfl  football player would take advantage of  me  i hope that you serve the 14 years and  it really impacts your life  if you ever come out i hope you get the  help that you need  in sign jane doe one  winslow's attorneys say that the one's  highest paid tight end in the nfl  suffers from traumatic brain injury from  playing football as well as the  motorcycle accident  which ended his career his  removal from the nfl is not his  inability to play in the nfl caused him  to drop into a deep deep spiral of  depression and anxiety and as a result  he saw medicated through alcohol  pain medication spice marijuana whatever  you can get a hold of  but that was his way of self-medicating  the reason all that is important  is it kind of dovetails into the old  thing in the room which is cte  we felt we felt that he's probably had  head injury at least a  thousand times and i think that the  conduct in this particular case  is supportive of this finding of cte or  potential cte  we know that cte can only be found when  the person is dead  in an autopsy but we can look at  symptoms  but he committed those crimes in 2003  before the nfl career  and before he suffered as you say the  thousand blows to the head  so it just leaves us with um  someone who can only be described in two  words and that  is a sexual predator and that's what  kellen winslow the second  is he's a sexual predator he  he preys on vulnerable victims and uh is  very brazen in the way he carries out  his crimes  back one last time to discuss the  sentencing of kelling wounds oh the  second is imran ansari  and terry austin during the sentencing  you just heard it the defense attorney  said  that winslow the keller winslow ii sorry  suffer from ct  could that have an effect maybe down the  road for some sort of an appeal  well brian you know that this condition  cte which is a essentially a traumatic  brain injury that  uh one develops um after repeated blows  to the head and here  uh it presents itself in football  players that was given as a reason  prior uh to the sentencing and i think a  22-page report that was filed with the  court last year  where the defense provided the court  with  uh medical evidence to show um how this  injury a traumatic brain injury can lead  to behavior  consistent with how winslow exhibited in  such as impulsive or erratic behavior  even to the degree of committing sexual  assault  so we have it here it's a negotiated  plea i don't think there's going to be  any appeal  but it was definitely used by the  defense in order  to mitigate the exposure for winslow um  in the sentencing phase uh and whether  that had an effect well  you know we see here that he has a  sentence which he was facing  life in prison here we have an assist a  sentence that was negotiated  and cte was a big part of that i believe  now tara we know with taking this plea  there's going to be no second trial  was this a right move considering the  victims don't need to take the stand  again  this was absolutely the right move  because these women will be spared  having to  testify again and we know that that was  very difficult for them  thank you both for joining us and thank  you for joining us here on crime daily  we'll see you next time  as we discuss justice in america  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

